165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101839 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1061 430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2bafd4a-88b6-500a-b45d-6de37cdf6804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1061 431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a5952ff-0a94-5306-8005-3e5b53635c33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101842 #edb/ref 1061 433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101842 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcca1c4b-c754-54c1-a87f-df2f0b306855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101842 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ -(x-1)^{3}+3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1065 236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "80db4900-d9c5-5900-b975-5c26b8f713e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1065 239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "416b850c-1c8d-5794-a787-3a59d48d0616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1067 "Limits of Piecewise Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/cb/cbdbe44a94ff036c74957691e30ed3652d50d2a8dbaa50b29827408f242492bb.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{1+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=k.$ So, $f(1)=k$, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1,$ we need to choose $k=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1065 548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/r