186115458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1045 "Continuity and Differentiability of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115458 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cf42fdc1-dee5-53a6-abe0-79ec4ef7b900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1061 615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62b549c4-9fa6-595c-9705-edecd57fab60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1061 616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d001457-3a19-5fb6-b582-e4447e917a71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116353 #edb/ref 1061 619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116353 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3a114b6-40b8-5189-a0c2-30250448ba57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116353 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1061 622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "872a7f94-c8a5-5f55-a91f-39909aca384f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1061 623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4284ae9c-1207-5fcc-829e-569528302322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **differentiable** at a point $x=a$ if the derivative $f'(a)$ exists.\n\nIn general:\n\n- If a function is not continuous at some point, then it is not differentiable at that point either.\n\n- If a function is differentiable at some point, then it must be continuous at that point.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given a step function $y=f(x)$ that has a jump discontinuity at $x=3,$ given below. We will verify that $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3$ by showing that $f'(3)$ does not exist. \n\n![](images/9f/9f91b034a27ce7fd2b432f0b094e4b40098247916b78f9c1b3eb64c7214f69b4.png)\n\nThe value $f'(3)$ is a limit, so to check whether it exists, we need to compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, we have that $f(x) = 2.$ So the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2-2}{x-3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, we have that $f(x)=1.$ So the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1-2}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{-1}{x-3}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}=\\text{DNE}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1065 332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60845968-f9d5-58f2-9205-7c60bc949ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1064 "If a function is continuous, that does *not* mean that it's differentiable!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $f(x)=|x|.$ The function $f(x)$ is continuous everywhere, including the point $x=0.$ But is $f(x)$ also differentiable everywhere? \n\n![](images/18/18644551713e0c1d451cf27bd43f3cc8fc8d7064d7af79df3cd2070af782c25e.png)\n\nFor $x < 0,$ the graph consists of a downwards-sloping line with slope $-1.$ For $x > 0,$ the graph consists of an upward-sloping line with slope $1.$ So, the derivative of the absolute value function is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1\\, & x<0, \\\\ 1\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the derivative of $f(x)=|x|$ does not exist at $x=0.$ This is because the limit of the slope as we approach $x=0$ from the left is not equal to the limit as we approach from the right.\n\nIn general, a continuous function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=a$ if the function $f(x)$ has any of the following features:\n\n- A **corner point** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=|x|$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **cusp** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x) = \\sqrt{|x|}$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **vertical tangent** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/28/28a6f3a424556e1e60e4959b03a5c38a1ff6a5d0efb110a0e5a4bfadc7072511.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1065 335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0df8723a-c6d6-562d-8cde-b44a47dd70bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1067 "Continuity Does Not Imply Differentiability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With One Quadratic Term by Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119937 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2072b915-15f9-5296-96dd-707e38df3d10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With Higher Order Terms by Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119938 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7eaa6cbd-46e1-5681-b536-06d16e8d3654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With Two Quadratic Terms by Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119942 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bea72566-55ba-582e-802c-e5ce5ef4a9fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Limits of Rational Functions by Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120021 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1cbd6cd3-5c23-5011-83d6-29ca3fbd3d32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120903 #edb/ref 1061 444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef3410b5-607f-5268-83e7-58a7aeef5936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120904 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120904 #edb/ref 1061 445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "213f9703-950a-5e68-9e6e-75e4744f5178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120905 #edb/ref 1061 448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b405cc4-9d67-53b0-94ff-33f5133c2609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120911 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120911 #edb/ref 1061 451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de1bdae6-79f1-521f-90b6-f0f8755e4df7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121006 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=0$, we get a confusing result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{x^{3} - 2x}{x^{2} + 4x} &= ((0)^{3} - 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nis called an **indeterminate form** because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} & = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\\ & = -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121006 #edb/ref 1065 246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121006 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "358e387e-1f79-5799-a6bc-d9a94fd46dd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121006 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124661 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124661 #edb